1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,320
Where does the buyer type their card number: on the shop's site, or on the bank's?

2
00:00:04,370 --> 00:00:11,184
If shops collected card numbers, every shop would need PCI DSS, and one shop's breach would leak cards.

3
00:00:11,234 --> 00:00:17,712
So the merchant sends the buyer to the bank's own payment page, through the one-time link it got when it created the payment.

4
00:00:17,762 --> 00:00:26,376
The link's token is the only credential: the database keeps only its hash, and after the configured time the link works no more.

5
00:00:26,426 --> 00:00:34,368
Reading the page changes nothing: it shows the website's name and logo, the amount, the order, and the cards the website accepts.

6
00:00:34,418 --> 00:00:42,312
The buyer types the card, and the BIN module names its scheme and bank first, so a card the website doesn't accept never gets a code.

7
00:00:42,362 --> 00:00:49,032
The page asks for the expiry as printed on the card, month then year, and names that format when the month is wrong.

8
00:00:49,082 --> 00:00:58,560
The card module checks the Luhn digit, stores the number encrypted, and asks the card's scheme, through its adapter, to send a one-time code to the cardholder's phone.

9
00:00:58,610 --> 00:01:07,416
A Humo card gets a code only when it is active and has a mobile banking phone, and a card Uzcard reports as blocked, lost or expired is never bound.

10
00:01:07,466 --> 00:01:13,488
Each code gets its own binding, so two payments with the same card never overwrite each other's code.

11
00:01:13,538 --> 00:01:20,880
The page now shows the bank, the scheme and the card's last four digits, kept in the payment's card columns, and waits for the code.

12
00:01:20,930 --> 00:01:26,064
The right code confirms the card through the same scheme, and the payment is ready to debit.

13
00:01:26,114 --> 00:01:32,712
Every attempt is counted, and committed before the scheme is asked, so a failed attempt still counts.

14
00:01:32,762 --> 00:01:38,592
Three cards, and three codes for each card, are allowed, and both numbers are configuration.

15
00:01:38,642 --> 00:01:41,976
When the last attempt fails, the page closes for good.

16
00:01:42,026 --> 00:01:52,680
One step runs at a time: a second click while the scheme is still answering is refused by a lock in Redis, which only its holder releases, and which outlives the slowest scheme call.

17
00:01:52,730 --> 00:01:56,016
Next, the moment the money actually moves: the debit.
